Nxd5 21. Be5 { Black was now hypnotized by his opponent’s great idea and failed to look at the position objectively. This is, of course, a forgivable mistake in a rapid game, but would not be so if we had it in a real game. } Qc5 $2 { Letting his opponent off the hook. Black could have won the game with } ( 21. hxg6 Bh3 $3 { 1 point } 22. Qe4 ( 22. Rxh3 Qe1 { is obvious. } ) 
Qa1 23. Kd2 Qxh1 24. Bxd5 Bb4 $3 25. Qxb4 Bxg2 { and wins. } ) 
22. Kd2 Qb4 23. Bc3 Qxc3 24. bxc3 Rd8 25. Nxf7 Nxc3 26. Nxd8 { 1–0 } ) 
 { The solution was 18 hxg6! } hxg6 ( 18... Nxc3 $6 19. gxf7 Rxf7 20. bxc3 Qxc3 21. Qc2 { would lead to a win for White. } ) 
19. Bxd5 $1 { 1 point } exd5 ( 19... Nxd5 $2 20. Rxd5 $1 exd5 21. Be5 { is mate. } ) 
20. Be5 { These are all simple moves. But sometimes this is what is takes. White is now threatening 21 Rh6 with the point 21...Bf5 22 Rdh1! } Bf5 { The other move that does not lose immediately is } ( 20... d4 { when White has a nice combination in } 21. Bxd4 Bf5 ( 21... Qxg5 22. Ne4 Bf5 23. Nxg5 Rfc8 24. Kd2 { gives White an extra pawn and better positioning of his pieces in the endgame. } ) 
22. Nge4 Kg7 { Obviously, we would stop here in our calculation – if we had even made it this far. } 23. g4 $1 Bxg4 24. Rdg1 Bf5 25. Rg5 $1 Rac8 26. Nxf6 Bxf6 27. Rxf5 $1 Qxf5 28. Qxf5 gxf5 29. Rg1 { White wins. } ) 
21. Rxd5 $1 { 2 points } Qb6 22. Qa2 { White has a winning attack and an extra pawn. Mistakes are still likely to happen from both sides, but it is a good starting point for White! } 
