 { Grischuk – Aronian, Ohrid 2009 } { _ } 26. e5 $1 { Grischuk found himself caught up in a nasty piece of home preparation and now had to look for a way out. In such a situation the method of elimination comes in handy. } ( 26. Be5 $2 Bd6 $3 { 3 points. This is the direct refutation. } 27. Bxf6 ( 27. Nc5 Bxc5 28. Rxg8 Qh6 29. dxc5 Qd2 { and Black wins. } ) 
Rxa8 28. Kf1 ( 28. Nxd6 Ra1 29. Kh2 Kxd6 { wins easily as well. The white pawns are all targets. } ) 
Ra1 29. Ke2 Ra2 { Grischuk resigned. 0–1 } ) 
 ( 26. d5 $2 { is quite easily refuted with } exd5 27. exd5 Bd6 $1 { and White is left stunned and forced to resign. } ) 
 ( 26. Na5 { is quite easy to get rid of, at least as an equalizing option. After } Qxd4 $1 { 1 point, White can play his idea } 27. Rd8 Kxd8 28. Nxc6 Kd7 29. Nxd4 { But after } Bd6 $17 { his position is just unpleasant. For example: } 30. e5 Bc5 ( 30. e5 Be7 31. Be4 $1 Kc7 32. Kf1 Rd8 33. Nc2 Rd1 34. Ke2 Rd2 35. Kf1 $11 ) 
31. Nc2 Ra8 { with the direct threat ...Ra3. Still White can fight on with } 32. Ne1 $1 Ra2 33. Kf1 Kc6 $15 { Progress is difficult for Black, but White’s position is unpleasant. } ) 
 ( 26. Ra7 $6 Qxd4 27. Nc5 Ke8 28. Ra8 Qd8 29. Rxd8 Kxd8 30. Na4 $17 { is also unpleasant for White. Compared to 26 Na5, he has a pawn less. So this can easily be dismissed. } ) 
 { The solution was therefore: 26 e5! } Qh6 27. Ra7 $1 { 4 points } ( 27. Na5 $2 { is refuted by bringing out the bishop. For example: } Bc5 $1 28. Rxg8 Qd2 29. dxc5 Qxd3 { and the c-pawn will draw blood. } ) 
Qc1+ ( 27... Kc7 28. Na5 Kb6 29. Ra6 $1 { with a draw. } ) 
28. Kh2 c2 29. Na5+ { With perpetual check after } Ke8 
